and more in this flash crash case and  what this guy did spoofing layering we  might need a crash course and what that  means exactly what these trades are  Bloomberg's Finance reporter Cary Geiger  has a quick explainer for us Cary hey  Betty  so spoofing is an interesting market  structure issue basically traders will  flood the market with the intention of  them with orders of a certain security  with the intention of them canceling  those orders and what that does is it  artificially drives the price of that  security up or down and it puts all the  other traders that are working you know  basically looking to trade that security  in a frenzy and it just creates a very  false price and demand and output for  that security and under the dodd-frank  law which was passed in 2010 smoothly  became very illegal so to speak and we  have not seen a lot of criminal activity  with spoofing cases we saw the first big  one last year under the US Attorney's  Office in Chicago with Michael Kousaka  so this case is another landmark  crinimal criminal spoofing case that  we're seeing coming out of the US  Attorney's Office now okay so so that's  spoofing what about layering Cary well  basically it's similar it's a similar  type of thing where you take different  orders you take you know basically you  flood the market with orders and  basically what you're trying to do is  hide your intentions on whether you're  going to buy and sell a specific stock  and again it creates a false sense of  whether that sock stock is going to be  bought or sold and then the market will  artificially drive that price up or down  so it's basically just another type of  market manipulation
 

